\@doanenote {190}
macro:->These
are
\emph
{loess}
model
fits
(locally-estimated
scatterplot
smoothing).
\@endanenote 
\@doanenote {191}
macro:->This
plot
reproduces
Figure
2
from
Hofmann,
Wickham,
and
Kafadar
(2017).
This
is
the
default
letter-value
plot
produced
by
the
R
package
\{lvplot\}
(Wickham
and
Hofmann
2025).
\@endanenote 
\@doanenote {192}
macro:->In
order
to
incorporate
as
many
points
as
possible,
this
analysis
just
focuses
on
the
default
\{lvplot\}
settings
that
are
used
in
Figure~\ref
{fig-lv-default}
and
even
sinks
to
the
level
of
critiquing
the
specific
data
set
(and
non-linear
scales)
that
are
used
in
this
specific
plot.
It
is
a
very
unfair
attack
on
letter-value
plots
in
general
and
the
\{lvplot\}
package
in
particular.
\@endanenote 
\@doanenote {193}
macro:->Letter
values
are
an
interesting
data
summary
in
the
sense
that
each
letter
value
corresponds
to
a
raw
data
value.
In
other
words,
letter
values
are
a
subset
of
the
original
raw
data
values,
chosen
to
represent
boundaries
beyond
which
a
certain
proportion
of
the
raw
data
values
lie.
\par
This
is
not
entirely
true
because
a
letter
value
may
fall
between
two
raw
data
values.
\@endanenote 
\@doanenote {194}
macro:->This
criticism
is
only
relevant
to
the
default
letter-value
plot.
A
variation
of
the
letter-value
plot
design
involves
a
different
encoding
of
width
so
that
the
area
of
the
rectangle
reflects
the
proportion
of
the
data
between
the
letter
values.
\@endanenote 
